MANDARIN HUB — TODO
===================

STUDENT PROFILE (read this first — it justifies every choice below)
------------------------------------------------------------------
  - Studied traditional characters for ~10 years, roughly 20 years ago.
    RECOGNIZES a lot of characters. Does NOT know their meanings, any grammar,
    or how to speak. Recognition is already strong; everything else is at zero.
  - Learned sounds via zhuyin/bopomofo (ㄅㄆㄇㄈ), never pinyin.
    Has not HEARD spoken Mandarin in ~20 years.
  - Destination is MAINLAND CHINA.
      Target script    = SIMPLIFIED
      Target phonetics = PINYIN
      Zhuyin is OUT OF SCOPE. Do not add zhuyin features. Do not add a zhuyin
      column. This was considered and explicitly rejected.
  - Traditional is kept for ONE reason only: as a recognition bridge. Seeing that
    a simplified character maps to a traditional one he already knows makes it feel
    familiar rather than brand-new. It does not give him meaning. So traditional is
    a secondary/reference display, never the primary.
  - GOAL: real conversation. Handle replies that weren't scripted. Recall the core
    phrases instantly, spoken, without gesture. Longer term: watch Netflix in
    Mandarin to re-train the ear.

  DESIGN CONSEQUENCE: he already owns recognition (character -> sound/meaning).
  Almost every existing drill trains that same direction, which is the muscle he
  least needs. New work should run the OTHER directions: sound -> meaning, and
  English -> spoken Chinese.


SETTLED DECISIONS — do not re-litigate these
--------------------------------------------
  - ZHUYIN / BOPOMOFO: out of scope. Destination is mainland China; the target
    notation is pinyin. Do not propose adding a zhuyin column or a zhuyin bridge.

  - HANDWRITING ON PAPER: KEEP IT. It was questioned on time-cost grounds (he'll
    type with a pinyin IME in China, so he may never handwrite in anger) and that
    objection was considered and REJECTED, correctly. The reasoning:
      Pinyin is a transcription, not a memory hook. In English or Spanish you see a
      word and the shape carries the meaning; a romanization of a sound carries
      nothing to hang meaning on. For this student the character IS the anchor —
      he already recognizes thousands of shapes, he just has no meaning attached
      to them. Writing the character is what binds meaning to a shape he already
      half-knows. Take that away and there is nothing left to remember the word BY.
    So the paper step stays. Any future "make it faster" proposal must preserve it.

  - TRADITIONAL: display-only, secondary. Bridge for recognition, never primary.


PRIORITY 1 — flip the direction of practice
-------------------------------------------

1. LISTENING-FIRST MODE   (new page: listening_drill.html)
   Why: he has never trained hear -> understand. This is the wall for both travel
   and the Netflix goal. Highest-value item on this list.

   Behaviour:
     - Play audio for a phrase FIRST. Render no Chinese text, no pinyin, no English
       until the user answers. (Critical: if text renders first it silently becomes
       another recognition drill and the whole point is lost.)
     - Show 4 English meanings; user picks one. Then reveal simplified + pinyin +
       English together, and replay the audio once on reveal.
     - Replay button, always available, unlimited replays before answering.
     - Wrong answers get pushed back into the queue ~5 cards later.
     - Second sub-mode, "tone drill": play a single word, ask which tone (1/2/3/4/neutral).
       Build minimal pairs from the existing phrase set where possible (mā/má/mǎ/mà).

   Implementation:
     - Audio: reuse the existing TTS approach — Audio() against
       translate.google.com/translate_tts (see speak() in mandarin_translation.html).
       The page MUST carry <meta name="referrer" content="no-referrer"> or Google
       returns 404. This is verified: with a Referer it 404s, without one it 200s.
     - Distractors: pull the 3 wrong options from the same lesson so they're
       plausible, not random.
     - Reuse the control bar from mandarin_flashcards_2.html (shuffle / speed /
       sound toggle) so it feels like the rest of the site.
     - Listen for the hub's postMessage {type:'mandarin-hub:hidden'} and stop audio,
       same as the other decks already do.
     - Add to index.html nav under "Practice", and to sw.js STATIC_ASSETS (bump CACHE_NAME).

2. PRODUCTION MODE   (UPGRADE mandarin_flashcards_2.html — do not build a new page)
   Note: Recall Mode ALREADY does this direction. Its header literally reads
   "English prompt - recall the Chinese", with a Show button calling revealAnswer().
   So prompted production exists. What's missing is the feedback loop and the grading.

   Why: he currently does English -> say aloud -> write on paper by hand, then hits
   Show. He never hears whether what he said was right. Closing that loop is the work.

   Behaviour to ADD:
     - On reveal, play the audio automatically so he can compare what he said to
       what it should sound like. (Today reveal is silent unless he clicks.)
     - Self-grade after reveal: [Again] / [Hard] / [Good]. Store the grades — this
       is the hook that spaced repetition (parking-lot item) will later read.
     - Optional "no pinyin" toggle so he can force himself off the crutch.

   Implementation:
     - Grades in localStorage under key `mandarin-production-grades`, shape
       { [phraseId]: {seen, again, hard, good, lastSeen} }.
     - Needs stable phrase IDs -> depends on item 3 below. Do item 3 first.


2B. TRANSFORM & GENERATE DRILLS  (the missing levels of production)
   Why: production has three levels. He only trains level 1.
     Level 1 RECALL    — English cue -> the one memorized phrase. = Recall Mode. Exists.
     Level 2 TRANSFORM — take a phrase he knows and bend it with grammar. MISSING.
     Level 3 GENERATE  — build a sentence he has never seen, fast, in response to
                         something unexpected. MISSING.
   Conversation happens at level 3. Level 1 alone produces the exact failure he
   described: knows the phrase, gets a reply off-script, freezes. The grammar guide
   explains all 15 patterns but never makes him USE one to build something new —
   that gap is what these two drills close.

   TRANSFORM DRILL (new page: transform_drill.html)
     - Show a phrase he already knows + one instruction. He says the transformed
       version aloud, then reveals.
         我要这个     "make it negative"      -> 我不要这个
         我要这个     "make it a question"    -> 我要这个吗? / 你要哪个?
         我去北京     "say you already went"  -> 我去过北京 / 我去了北京
         他是老师     "say he isn't"          -> 他不是老师
         这个好       "compare with that one" -> 这个比那个好
     - Instruction set maps 1:1 onto the 15 patterns already written up in
       grammar_guide.html (是...的, 了, 在+verb, 过, 想/要/会/能/可以, 把, 被,
       的/得/地, 比, connectors, 越来越, 一边...一边). Reuse those, do not invent new grammar.
     - Data: a transformations table { baseId, instruction, answer, patternRef }.
       baseId points into window.PHRASES (item 3), patternRef links to the anchor in
       grammar_guide.html so he can jump to the explanation when he gets it wrong.
     - Reveal plays audio + self-grade, same as the Recall Mode upgrade in item 2.

   GENERATE DRILL (new page: generate_drill.html)
     - Give an English SITUATION that is deliberately NOT in the phrase list, plus
       3-4 words he already knows. He builds a sentence from scratch, out loud.
         "The waiter asks if you want it spicy. You want it a little spicy."
           given: 要 / 一点 / 辣 / 不太
     - Reveal a MODEL answer, clearly labelled as one valid answer, not the only one.
       Plus audio. Self-graded — there is no way to auto-mark this and that is fine.
     - Timed variant: 5-second cap before reveal, to push toward automaticity.
       Speed is the whole point at level 3; a correct sentence after 20 seconds of
       assembly is not conversation.
     - Situations should be drawn from the same travel contexts as Course 1 so the
       vocabulary is already in place and only the assembly is new.


PRIORITY 2 — consistency and plumbing
-------------------------------------

3. SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH FOR PHRASE DATA
   Problem: the 227 phrases exist as `const DATA = [...]` inside
   mandarin_flashcards.html AND again inside mandarin_flashcards_2.html, and the
   14-day course re-states the same content as hand-written HTML tables. Three
   copies that drift.

   Fix:
     - Create data/phrases.js containing:  window.PHRASES = [ ... ];
       Shape per phrase: { id, lesson, section, simp, trad, pinyin, meaning }
       Add a stable `id` (e.g. "L01-005") — production mode and SRS both need it.
     - IMPORTANT: it must be a CLASSIC .js file that assigns a global. Do NOT make
       it a .json loaded with fetch() — fetch of a local file is blocked under
       file:// (CORS), which would break local use.
     - Update mandarin_flashcards.html and mandarin_flashcards_2.html to read
       window.PHRASES instead of their own DATA.
     - Add data/phrases.js to sw.js STATIC_ASSETS and bump CACHE_NAME.

4. GLOBAL SIMPLIFIED / TRADITIONAL SWITCH
   Problem: mandarin_flashcards_2.html and grammar_flashcards.html have a
   trad/simp toggle. mandarin_flashcards.html, mandarin_in_14_days.html and
   grammar_guide.html do NOT. So the script the student sees is inconsistent
   depending on which page he's on.

   Fix:
     - One control in the hub sidebar. Default SIMPLIFIED (that's the target script).
     - Store in localStorage as `mandarin-hub-script` = 'simp' | 'trad'.
     - Broadcast to pages via postMessage {type:'mandarin-hub:script', value:'simp'},
       mirroring the existing 'mandarin-hub:hidden' message. Each page listens and
       re-renders from the trad/simp fields it already has.
     - Use postMessage as the transport, NOT shared localStorage. Under file:// the
       iframes are cross-origin/opaque, and it is not confirmed they share the hub's
       localStorage (observed: `mandarin-hub-speed` was present on the https origin
       but absent on file://). postMessage works on both.
     - Also send the current value on iframe load, so a newly-opened page starts correct.

5. FIX THE 14-DAY COURSE
   Problems found:
     - Mixed scripts in one document: 這 x6 vs 这 x12, 說 x5 vs 说 x8, 個 x8 vs 个 x18.
       Same drift in grammar_guide.html (這 x7 / 这 x9, 說 x2 / 说 x4, 會 x5 / 会 x7).
       For a student whose only foothold is character shape, this is actively confusing.
     - The course is hand-written HTML tables, so it can't participate in item 4 and
       drifts from the flashcard data.
   Fix:
     - Normalize to SIMPLIFIED as the printed script throughout.
     - Then render the lesson phrase tables from window.PHRASES (item 3) so the
       course, the deck and Recall Mode can never disagree again, and so the global
       script switch (item 4) reaches the course too.
     - Keep the prose/explanations as hand-written HTML — only the phrase tables
       become data-driven.


