   Open: decide the real usage order before moving anything. Candidate order is
   Translate -> Practice -> Courses -> Grammar -> Pronunciation -> S/T, on the
   theory that S/T is reference material consulted rarely, while Courses is daily.
   Do not reorder on a guess; confirm first.


PRIORITY 4 — new content
------------------------

7. COURSE 2 — "HANDLING THE REPLY"   (was: "Next 14-Day Course")
   STATUS: AGREED. Course 2 is the same travel situations taught from the OTHER side
   of the conversation — what he HEARS back — not deeper vocabulary on the same topics.

   The failure it fixes, concretely:
     Him:    我要这个            ("I want this one")  <- Course 1 taught him this. Perfect.
     Waiter: 在这儿吃还是带走?    ("eat here or take away?")
     Him:    ...frozen.
   His Mandarin didn't fail. Course 1 taught him every sentence he SAYS and not one
   sentence he HEARS. Course 2 closes that.

   Context: Course 1 answers "can he survive 14 days in China with just this book?"
   It teaches him to SPEAK first — 200-odd phrases he initiates. The gap is that in
   a real exchange the other person answers, and nothing prepares him for that.
   His stated goal is exactly this: handle replies that weren't scripted.

   So Course 2 should NOT be "the same lessons but harder vocabulary". It should be
   the same situations from the OTHER side of the counter.

   Per topic (start with the highest-traffic three: Ordering Food, Shopping,
   Directions), each lesson covers:
     - The 5-10 replies he will actually hear, as audio-first drills. e.g. for food:
       几位? / 在这儿吃还是带走? / 要不要辣? / 还要别的吗? / 扫码点单
     - The 2-3 ways each reply gets phrased, because real people vary the wording.
     - His response options for each.
     - Repair phrases — the highest-value item in the whole course and currently
       absent everywhere:
         请再说一遍  (please say it again)
         说慢一点    (slower please)
         我听不懂    (I don't understand)
         这个用中文怎么说? (how do you say this in Chinese?)
       These are what convert a dead-end into a conversation. They should arguably
       be pulled forward into Course 1 as well.
     - A branching mini-dialogue: his line -> one of 2-3 possible replies -> his
       next line. Even a simple scripted branch beats a linear dialogue for teaching
       him that replies vary.
   Format: audio-first, matching item 1. He should hear the reply before seeing it.


